d}x+3y & =6 \\\\ 3y & =-x+6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=3x$ has a slope of $3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=3x$ is perpendicular to the given line." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-23621/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-23621"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-44514"] :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $12x-4y=5,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-4y & =5 \\\\ -4y & =-12x+5 \\\\ y & =3x-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $3.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+3$ has a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+3$ is perpendicular to the given line." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-44515/selection/1f5b353af7cbb90a89e4a07f196cff5b0ba27d9934d00e9a38de61eff80108f7" :answer/id #uuid "3d24eb7f-d906-5b33-a5e2-7b40d57ee98d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-44515/selection/0aae0f62f78e37f51f0f104e1616f1edea4079865fd21fc152944066b39dedfe" :answer/id #uuid "2c7a120a-580a-52f3-acf2-eba54fdc98a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{9}" :